A compound noun is made of two or more words joined together (like "son-in-law" or "girl student"). 
To make it plural, you add s/es to the MAIN/HEAD word — not always the last word.

This trips people up because the instinct is to just add "s" to the end of the whole compound word — but that's often wrong. 
You have to identify which word is the actual core noun being pluralized.

                                                  Singular	                            Plural
                                                  Son-in-law	                        Sons-in-law
                                                  Brother-in-law	                    Brothers-in-law
                                                  Commander-in-chief	                Commanders-in-chief
                                                  Passer by                          	Passers by
                                                  Onlooker	                          Onlookers / lookers-on
                                                  Maid-servant	                      Maid-servants
                                                  Step-daughter	                      Step-daughters
                                                  Book fair	                          Book fairs
                                                  Spoonful	                          Spoonfuls
                                                  Member of Parliament	              Members of Parliament
                                                  Chief Minister	                    Chief Ministers
                                                  Landlord	                          Landlords
                                                  Girl student	                      Girl students
                                                  Man athlete                        	Men-athletes
                                                  Woman athlete                      	Women-athletes
                                                  Man servant                        	Men-servants
                                                  Man hater	                          Man haters
                                                  Woman lover	                        Woman lovers
                                                  Bed-room	                          Bed-rooms
                                                  Book-shelf	                        Book-shelves
                                                  Man-of-war	                        Men-of-war


Bonus Notes (from the same source, closely tied to this rule)

"Sister-in-law" plural in a sentence:

❌ All his sister in laws are married and settled in London.
✅ All his sisters-in-law are married and settled in London.

Tricky possessive + plural combo:

The commander-in-chief's orders arrived late... (possessive 's attaches to the END of the whole compound noun, not to the core/head word in the middle)
I attended my sister-in-law's marriage yesterday. (same idea — apostrophe goes on  the end of the compound)
